{"parentUuid":"e76355e7-a5e2-453c-941f-f8ff9c2d2898","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013VnUQWAtFxNvPJb2owCzGy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Quick explainer:\n\n**Partial clone** is a git feature for huge repos. When you clone with `--filter=blob:none` (which your `origin` is configured with — `remote.origin.partialclonefilter=blob:none`), git downloads commits and trees but skips file contents (blobs). The blobs get fetched lazily when something actually needs them (`git checkout`, `git diff`, etc.).\n\nA **promisor pack** is the on-disk record of \"these are the objects that came from a partial-clone-aware remote, which has *promised* to serve any missing dependencies on demand.\" Mechanics:\n\n- For every pack file `pack-abc.pack` / `pack-abc.idx`, git can drop an empty sibling file `pack-abc.promisor` next to it.\n- The `.promisor` marker tells git: objects in this pack came from a filtered fetch, and the remote (the *promisor remote*) will provide any objects that turn out to be missing.\n\nSo a \"promisor pack\" isn't a different file format — it's a normal pack plus an empty marker file. The marker is a signal that says \"filtered-clone semantics apply here.\"\n\nIn your repo:\n\n```\npack-969b3b5743d5a2352920c5fa8aaaedb08101cdb5.pack   ← 1.6 GB of trees+commits\npack-969b3b5743d5a2352920c5fa8aaaedb08101cdb5.idx    ← index\npack-969b3b5743d5a2352920c5fa8aaaedb08101cdb5.promisor   ← 0 bytes, just the marker\n```\n\nThat pack contains the `v2/main` tree structure. The `.promisor` marker means \"trust this; if any referenced object inside is missing, git will fetch it from `cli-checkpoints.git`.\"\n\n**Why it matters for your bug:** native `git` understands promisor semantics — when it needs an object, it checks local packs, and if missing, calls the promisor remote. go-git **doesn't** implement promisor support. It scans your `.git/objects/pack/` directory at startup, builds an in-memory